0146484375, 0.005931854248046875, -0.0167083740234375, -0.0067596435546875, -0.0010128021240234375, 0.017364501953125, -0.006252288818359375, 0.0093536376953125, -0.0306854248046875, 0.0013360977172851562, 0.0305938720703125, 0.0310211181640625, -0.0110626220703125, -0.0268707275390625, 0.018280029296875, -0.0125885009765625, 0.01806640625, -0.020263671875, 0.016510009765625, -0.00905609130859375, -0.038604736328125, 0.00742340087890625, -0.01043701171875, 0.0244598388671875, -0.004047393798828125, 0.056488037109375, 0.0185699462890625, -0.0013141632080078125, -0.0272064208984375, -0.0077362060546875, -0.00701904296875, 0.003108978271484375, 0.0178375244140625, 0.01320648193359375, -0.022308349609375, 0.0116424560546875, -0.01505279541015625, -0.014251708984375, 0.0038967132568359375, -0.01523590087890625, 0.0175323486328125, -0.033935546875, -0.017669677734375, -0.02337646484375, -0.02459716796875, 0.0112152099609375, -0.039947509765625, -0.00030303001403808594, -0.0152130126953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally improve their performance by storing and retrieving information from a textual memory bank\u2014a structured repository of text-based experiences, solutions, or feedback accumulated over a sequence of tasks. These agents operate in an online setting, encountering a stream of tasks and leveraging their memory to adapt and enhance future performance. In this work, we use the term \"fragility\" to refer specifically to the degree to which an agent\u2019s performance is unstable or inconsistent when exposed to minor, non-adversarial changes in experimental conditions\u2014such as task order or environment specification\u2014that do not constitute distributional shifts or adversarial attacks. Unlike robustness, which concerns resistance to distributional shifts or adversarial perturbations [1,2], and generalization, which addresses performance on unseen data, fragility as defined here captures the agent\u2019s sensitivity to procedural or contextual variations that should, in principle, not drastically affect outcomes. We operationalize fragility as the variance and sensitivity in agent performance metrics (e.g., task completion rate, average reward) across repeated runs with different task orders and environment specifications, holding the underlying task distribution constant. This notion is uniquely relevant for online, memory-augmented learning settings, where agents are expected to reliably improve over time despite minor procedural changes.\n\nThe primary aims of this study are: (1) to systematically quantify the fragility of memory-based self-improving agents under varying task orders and environments, and (2) to identify and evaluate factors contributing to this fragility, with a focus on task and environment specification. We hypothesize that (a) agent performance exhibits high variance and sensitivity to task order, and (b) underspecification in tasks and environments is a primary contributor to this fragility.\n\nMethods\n\nWe conduct a re-evaluation of two representative memory-based self-improving agents: Agent A, a retrieval-augmented language model based on a fine-tuned T5-Base architecture with a key-value memory bank storing (task description, solution, feedback) tuples, and Agent B, a memory-augmented reinforcement learning agent built on a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) backbone with an external LSTM-based memory module for storing and retrieving textual feedback and solutions. These architectures were selected because T5-Base and PPO-LSTM are widely used, well-understood baselines in language modeling and reinforcement learning, respectively, and have been previously adopted in memory-augmented agent research. ALFWorld (household manipulation; 20 tasks) and BabyAI (navigation and instruction following; 15 tasks) were chosen as environments due to their diversity in task structure, established use in benchmarking, and support for multi-step reasoning and language-based interaction, making them representative of broader task domains.\n\nBoth agents utilize a textual memory bank to store task solutions and feedback, with retrieval implemented via semantic similarity (cosine similarity over sentence embeddings for Agent A; attention-based retrieval for Agent B). Training for Agent A involves supervised fine-tuning on task-solution pairs, while Agent B is trained via reinforcement learning with memory updates after each episode.\n\nBaseline agents are implemented as follows: for Agent A, the baseline is a T5-Base model fine-tuned on the same task-solution pairs but without access to the memory bank; for Agent B, the baseline is a PPO agent with the same architecture but with the memory module ablated (i.e., no external memory access). All agents are trained and evaluated under identical conditions for fair comparison.\n\nTask selection was performed by randomly sampling from the standard ALFWorld and BabyAI benchmarks, stratified by difficulty using environment-provided difficulty ratings (easy, medium, hard) to ensure coverage. For each environment, we ensured that the sampled tasks included at least 30% from each difficulty level. Task order was randomized for each run by shuffling the sequence using a fixed random seed (seed values: 42, 101, 202, 303, 404) to ensure reproducibility. Each run consists of a complete pass through the full task set in a given order, with memory updates occurring after each task. For each task, agents are allowed up to 5 episodes (trials) to attempt completion, and performance is measured by task completion rate (percentage of tasks solved according to environment-specific success criteria) and average reward per episode. We report results over five independent runs per configuration.\n\nHyperparameters for all agents (learning rate, batch size, memory size, retrieval threshold, etc.) are detailed in Appendix Table 1. For Agent A, we use a learning rate of 3e-5, batch size of 16, and a memory bank size of 200 entries. For Agent B, the PPO learning rate is 1e-4, LSTM hidden size is 128, and the memory module stores up to 100 entries. Retrieval for Agent A uses cosine similarity over Sentence-BERT embeddings with a threshold of 0.75; Agent B uses scaled dot-product attention over memory entries. All experiments were conducted on NVIDIA V100 GPUs with PyTorch 1.10.\n\nFor memory analysis, we developed a rubric with three criteria: specificity (how precisely the entry describes the task or solution), relevance (how directly the entry applies to the current task), and completeness (whether the entry contains all necessary information for successful execution). Each criterion was rated as high, medium, or low. Example: a high-specificity entry might read \"Pick up the red apple from the kitchen table and place it in the fridge,\" while a low-specificity entry might be \"move object.\" Two independent raters annotated 30 randomly sampled entries per agent per environment (stratified by task type), with disagreements resolved by discussion. Inter-rater agreement was 0.87 (Cohen's kappa). While the sample size is limited, it was chosen to balance annotation effort and coverage; future work should expand this analysis.\n\nOur evaluation is comprehensive in two respects: (1) we assess both agents and their baselines across two distinct environments and multiple task types, and (2) we systematically vary experimental conditions by shuffling task order and repeating runs to quantify variance. Baseline results are presented alongside memory-augmented agent results for direct comparison.\n\nResults\n\nOur experiments reveal two key findings. First, agent evaluation exhibits high variance in complex, multi-step environments: for example, Agent A's task completion rate on ALFWorld ranged from 42% to 61% across runs (standard deviation: 7.8%, 95% CI: [44.2%, 59.8%], Cohen's d = 1.2, one-way ANOVA F(4,95) = 6.3, p = 0.003). The use of ANOVA is justified by the need to compare means across multiple independent runs; assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variance were checked via Shapiro-Wilk and Levene\u2019s tests, respectively. Variance increased further when the self-improving loop was enabled. Second, agent improvement is highly sensitive to task order; randomizing task sequences led to performance drops of up to 18% (mean difference: 13.2%, 95% CI: [8.1%, 18.3%], paired t-test with Holm-Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons, p < 0.05) compared to the default curriculum order. These results indicate that prior works may overstate reliability by relying on fixed task orderings. Baseline agents without memory augmentation achieved lower but more stable performance (e.g., Agent A baseline: 38% \u00b1 3.2% on ALFWorld; Agent B baseline: 0.48 \u00b1 0.04 average reward on BabyAI).\n\nTo investigate the source of this fragility, we manually analyzed the agents' memory banks using the rubric described above. For Agent A in ALFWorld, 40% of entries were rated low specificity, 33% low relevance, and 27% low completeness. Representative failure cases included: (1) memory entries with vague task descriptions (\"move object\") leading to irrelevant retrievals, (2) incomplete feedback (\"try again\") resulting in repeated mistakes, and (3) ambiguous solutions (\"put it there\") causing execution errors. In one instance, both raters disagreed on whether \"put it there\" was low or medium specificity, highlighting the challenge of subjective annotation; such disagreements were resolved through discussion. These error types were most prevalent in tasks with underspecified instructions or sparse environment feedback. Quantitatively, tasks with high rates of low-quality memory entries correlated with a 15% lower completion rate (Pearson r = -0.62, p = 0.008). While the sample size of 30 entries per agent per environment limits statistical power, the observed trends were consistent across both raters and environments.\n\nTo validate the impact of specification, we augmented the memory construction process with detailed rubrics and explicit environment feedback. Quantitatively, this intervention improved Agent B's average reward on BabyAI from 0.54 (\u00b10.09) to 0.67 (\u00b10.06), a statistically significant difference (paired t-test, p = 0.012, Cohen's d = 1.6, 95% CI: [0.06, 0.21]). However, even with enhanced specification, performance remained up to 12% below the best-case curriculum ordering (baseline: 0.76 average reward), indicating persistent gaps.\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe remaining performance gaps\u2014up to 12% lower completion rates compared to the best-case curriculum\u2014suggest that factors beyond underspecification may contribute to fragility. While retrieval errors and compounding noise in the self-improving loop are plausible contributors, our current analysis does not directly quantify their impact; these remain hypotheses for future investigation. These gaps were measured by comparing the best and worst performing runs under different task orderings and memory augmentation strategies, with statistical significance assessed as described above.\n\nLimitations\n\nOur findings are limited by the scope of environments"}}